and basic cell culture.

00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:35.633
So for the brief introduction,

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.133
the i-Series come in two
different formats -

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.000
the i5, shown on the left,
has a single pod

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:43.267
which can either have
a multichannel head,

00:03:43.333 --> 00:03:46.367
such as a 96 or a 384 head,

00:03:46.433 --> 00:03:48.533
or it can have a Span-8 pipetters,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.267
which each of the
probes can spread out,

00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:54.033
allowing you to
access multiple tubes at once.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.567
Shown on the right
is the Biomek i7.

00:03:56.633 --> 00:03:58.567
This can have up to two heads,

00:03:58.633 --> 00:04:01.400
so it can have either a single
multichannel or a single Span-8 head,

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.800
or it can have dual
multichannels or one of each -

00:04:03.867 --> 00:04:05.900
a Span-8 and a multichannel.

00:04:05.967 --> 00:04:10.667
It obviously has a larger footprint
than the i5 shown on the left.

00:04:10.967 --> 00:04:14.167
Some of the new features that
are shown on the i-Series

00:04:14.233 --> 00:04:18.267
include a status light, shown at the
top of the enclosure to the right.

00:04:18.333 --> 00:04:21.167
This light can basically indicate what
is happening while on the system,

00:04:21.233 --> 00:04:24.533
whether it's currently running, whether
it needs to have an interaction,

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.167
or whether it's in standby mode.

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.300
And there's also a new deck LED light,

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:33.900
which helps illuminate the
deck for configuring the deck,

00:04:33.967 --> 00:04:36.133
as well as just keeping
an eye on what's happening.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:37.933
However, this light can be turned off

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.100
if you're working with
light-sensitive reagents.

00:04:40.167 --> 00:04:42.700
There's also an optional
enclosure shown to the right,

00:04:42.767 --> 00:04:45.300
and this very useful for
cell culture applications

00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:47.933
because it can be configured
with HEPA filtered fans

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.733
to ensure a sterile
working environment.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.400
It also can enable direct integrations,
also shown to the right,

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:56.933
and we'll cover a number
of these throughout the talk,

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:02.833
whether it be plate readers,
incubators, flow cytometers, etc.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.133
Both of the instruments
have a configurable high-density deck,

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.967
shown on the lower left.

00:05:08.033 --> 00:05:12.200
The Biomek i5 deck has up to
25 microtiter plate positions.

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:15.433
And the i7 has up to
45 microtiter plate positions.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.400
These can be
configured in different ways

00:05:17.467 --> 00:05:19.867
with different positions
for heating and cooling,

00:05:19.933 --> 00:05:22.700
for trash or washes, etc.

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:26.333
And each of the systems features
a high-axis rotating gripper.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.567
This allows complete
coverage of the deck

00:05:28.633 --> 00:05:30.633
so that all positions can
be accessed.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.400
And because of the high access,

00:05:32.467 --> 00:05:35.167
you can also pick up a microtiter plate
next to a taller piece of labware,

00:05:35.233 --> 00:05:37.033
such as a tip box.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.267
There's one per head,

00:05:38.333 --> 00:05:40.500
so if you have two
heads on the system,

00:05:40.567 --> 00:05:42.100
you would have two grippers.

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:45.333
When it comes to liquid handlers,

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.100
obviously, liquid handing
is still the key,

00:05:47.167 --> 00:05:49.633
so we have made some improvements
to the multichannel head.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:51.467
One of the main features is

00:05:51.533 --> 00:05:54.367
that there's now a 1,200
microliter capacity head.

00:05:54.433 --> 00:05:58.867
This enables you to
transfer one mil per tip

00:05:58.933 --> 00:06:02.200
while also having a 20 percent
additional blowout

00:06:02.267 --> 00:06:04.433
to ensure all the liquid
is removed from the tips.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.533
And the head now
features selective tip pipetting,

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.733
so enabling you to pick up
a single column, a single row,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.000
or a single tip or any
combination of tips as needed.

00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:15.100
This obviously gives
a lot more flexibility

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:20.267
than being forced to always use
96 or 384 tips at once.

00:06:20.333 --> 00:06:23.867
On the Span-8 side, we now
have per probe calibration.

00:06:23.933 --> 00:06:25.900
So obviously, we know there may be

00:06:25.967 --> 00:06:28.033
some slight inherent
differences between the pumps,

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.067
so this really enables you

00:06:30.133 --> 00:06:32.800
to dial in the accuracy
of the low end if needed.

00:06:32.867 --> 00:06:35.933
So obviously, having the ability
to calibrate each probe

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.100
can really provide some of
that additional precision.

00:06:39.167 --> 00:06:43.333
And also, from a simplicity standpoint,
we now have a volume spitting feature,

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.000
that all you have to do
is check a check box,

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:49.033
and if you ask to transfer a volume
that is more than the tip capacity

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.033
or the head capacity,

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:53.033
the Biomek will do the calculations

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:58.167
and split it into the number of
transfers needed to reach that volume.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.633
We also have a deck
observation system.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.000
This basically entails
two wide-angle cameras,

00:07:04.067 --> 00:07:06.967
one on each of the
front corners of the system.

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:09.367
This can enable remote monitoring.

00:07:09.433 --> 00:07:11.367
Obviously, for longer work loads,

00:07:11.433 --> 00:07:13.800
you may not necessarily want to
stay by the instrument,

00:07:13.867 --> 00:07:16.033
so this allows you to monitor

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:19.900
what's happening on the instrument from
your desktop or your laptop computer.

00:07:19.967 --> 00:07:23.300
Another feature is that it
will record 30 seconds on both

00:07:23.367 --> 00:07:25.733
before and after any error that occurs.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.267
Obviously, a good way to troubleshoot
if something has gone wrong.

00:07:30.333 --> 00:07:33.400
We also offer the PROService
Remote Management System.

00:07:33.467 --> 00:07:37.867
This can actually allow our tech support
folks to access some of the error logs

00:07:37.933 --> 00:07:41.200
or possibly look at some of
those minute long videos

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:45.233
to try to get an idea of what, if any,
maintenance is needed for the system.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.633
And this should enable them to
bring the parts they would need

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.800
to help save an additional trip.

00:07:49.867 --> 00:07:52.133
And finally, the system
is Windows 10 compatible,

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.167
which is great for moving forward

00:07:54.233 --> 00:07:57.900
as more and more instruments
will be running on Windows 10.

00:07:58.633 --> 00:08:01.100
So moving now into
the cellular applications.

00:08:01.167 --> 00:08:04.067
We'll start out with an ELISA method.

00:08:04.133 --> 00:08:06.267
I think most people are
familiar with ELISAs.

00:08:06.333 --> 00:08:08.333
It's obviously pretty ubiquitous.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.867
It is a fairly labor-intensive
process though,

00:08:10.933 --> 00:08:12.900
and shown on the lower left hand side

00:08:12.967 --> 00:08:15.867
is just a workflow that we
use for our proof of principle work.

00:08:15.933 --> 00:08:20.067
This was an IL-22 ELISA kit that
was just commercially available.

00:08:20.133 --> 00:08:22.867
And the steps include
doing a serial dilution,

00:08:22.933 --> 00:08:24.633
using the automation,

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:27.700
and then adding a diluent and the
samples and standards to the plate,

00:08:27.767 --> 00:08:30.633
shaking it for two hours,
doing four washes,

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.433
adding a conjugate to the plate, and
then shaking for another two hours,

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.367
followed by additional washes,

00:08:36.433 --> 00:08:38.967
and then a 30-minute incubation
with a substrate,

00:08:39.033 --> 00:08:41.100
adding a stop solution,
and then reading absorbents.

00:08:41.167 --> 00:08:43.367
So a fairly straightforward workflow,

00:08:43.433 --> 00:08:45.300
but again, one that if done manually

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:47.400
can essentially take the good
portion of the day

00:08:47.467 --> 00:08:51.033
and just requires quite
a number of liquid transfers.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:54.500
Shown on the lower right hand
side is the deck of the i5.

00:08:54.567 --> 00:08:57.000
If you wanted to use
fresh tips for each transfer,

00:08:57.067 --> 00:08:59.100
you can see very quickl
how the deck fills up,

00:08:59.167 --> 00:09:01.933
and this is one of the advantages
of the high capacity deck,

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.300
is that we're able to incorporate all
this labware during the workflow.

00:09:06.367 --> 00:09:08.600
Another one of the
challenges for ELISAs is

00:09:08.667 --> 00:09:11.767
that it's frequently very
highly variable in throughput.

00:09:11.833 --> 00:09:15.833
The same workflow can be used
for assaying 8 to 24 samples,

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:18.233
or it could be a much higher
throughput type of application

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.867
where you're screening for
IGD producing clones,

00:09:20.933 --> 00:09:24.400
where it can be in the hundreds
to thousands of samples.

00:09:24.467 --> 00:09:29.200
We'll keep this in mind as we
talk about more of the workflow.

00:09:29.267 --> 00:09:30.900
So for the proof of principle,

00:09:30.967 --> 00:09:35.567
we used the Biomek i5's Span-8
system, shown to the right.

00:09:35.633 --> 00:09:38.567
We were able to process a full
plate in six and a half hours.

00:09:38.633 --> 00:09:40.533
And obviously, using the Span-8 system,

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.633
this would be more of a low
to medium throughput,

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.900
probably one to two
plates a day.

00:09:44.967 --> 00:09:46.867
Of that six and a half hour workflow,

00:09:46.933 --> 00:09:49.933
four and a half hours were just
straight incubations.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.533
And obviously, when
we do ELISAs manually,

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:54.867
typically this involves
adding wash buffer

00:09:54.933 --> 00:09:57.267
and inverting the plate over some waste

00:09:57.333 --> 00:09:59.733
and shaking it out
and blotting it against a pad.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.933
This is not something that's
very automation friendly,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.100
so to ensure that we have removed
all the liquid from the plates,

00:10:06.167 --> 00:10:08.067
we use our optimized
by pipetting techniques

00:10:08.133 --> 00:10:11.767
to aspirate around the
circumference of the well,

00:10:11.833 --> 00:10:14.467
sort of at the north, south,
east, and west quadrants,

00:10:14.533 --> 00:10:16.533
and just ensure that we were
able to remove

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:18.767
all the liquid
from the flat bottom wells.

00:10:18.833 --> 00:10:21.800
We were able to achieve
excellent standard curves

00:10:21.867 --> 00:10:24.800
as shown by the precision of our -

00:10:24.867 --> 00:10:27.400
the reproducibility of
our triplicate values

00:10:27.467 --> 00:10:29.933
on our standard curves shown on
the lower left hand side,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.300
where all the CVs of our
replicates were under five percent.

00:10:33.367 --> 00:10:35.467
And we also had excellent linearity

00:10:35.533 --> 00:10:39.167
as indicated by R² value
of .991 for our standard curve,

00:10:39.233 --> 00:10:43.367
indicating our automated serial
dilution was highly effective.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.600
So this is a fairly
straightforward application,

00:10:47.667 --> 00:10:51.133
but if you want to do those types
of higher throughput applications

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:53.833
that I mentioned earlier,
such as clone screening,

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:57.133
there are a number of considerations
when moving to that higher throughput.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:00.100
The first is that you'll likely need
to move to a multichannel system.

00:11:00.167 --> 00:11:02.667
The nice thing with having
the selective tip feature

00:11:02.733 --> 00:11:05.867
is that even if you're only
assaying 24 or 8 samples,

00:11:05.933 --> 00:11:08.667
this still can be done
using the multichannel head

00:11:08.733 --> 00:11:10.467
using the selective tip feature.

00:11:10.533 --> 00:11:12.300
Another consideration is deck space.

00:11:12.367 --> 00:11:14.967
As I showed before, this
could require a lot of tips,

00:11:15.033 --> 00:11:18.633
a lot of buffers or reservoirs,
that's for a single plate.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:20.533
So if you have additional plates,

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:23.700
having the higher deck capacity
of an i7 might be valuable,

00:11:23.767 --> 00:11:26.433
or you might need to integrate
some additional storage positions,

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.967
and we have labware feeders
that will allow you

00:11:29.033 --> 00:11:34.433
to have lots of tips or plates
available for a higher throughput.

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.333
Another consideration is dead volume.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:39.200
Obviously, if you're using a
multichannel reservoir,

00:11:39.267 --> 00:11:41.533
the dead volume for those are
roughly 20 mils,

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:44.000
which could obviously be more
than you would have available

00:11:44.067 --> 00:11:46.500
for some of the reagents
such as antibodies,

00:11:46.567 --> 00:11:50.000
but wash buffer etc
might be very plentiful.

00:11:50.067 --> 00:11:52.033
So you would need to consider

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:54.800
the balance between speed
and throughput

00:11:54.867 --> 00:11:56.933
versus how much reagent
you have available.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.233
And again, using the selective
tip feature,

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:01.733
you could go ahead and use that
for the antibodies well

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.667
using all 96 channels for the wash.

00:12:04.733 --> 00:12:07.200
Another consideration is scheduling.

00:12:07.267 --> 00:12:09.333
Obviously, if you're processing
multiple plates

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:11.633
and if you want to compare those
results across the plates,

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:14.167
you need to ensure you
have consistent timing.

00:12:14.233 --> 00:12:17.467
This may not be so essential
for two hour incubation,

00:12:17.533 --> 00:12:20.733
but some of those substrate
incubations at the end of the ELISAs

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.367
may call for only five to ten minutes,
possibly as low as two minutes,

00:12:24.433 --> 00:12:28.367
before you are required to
add your stop solution,

00:12:28.433 --> 00:12:31.767
and for this, you obviously want
to put some timing constraints in,

00:12:31.833 --> 00:12:34.800
and that's one of the nice features
of our SAMI EX scheduling software,

00:12:34.867 --> 00:12:37.000
shown on the lower right hand corner.

00:12:37.067 --> 00:12:39.233
You can allow the software

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:42.200
to try to schedule the plates
as efficiently as possible,

00:12:42.267 --> 00:12:45.100
but for some of those critical steps,
you are able to put in constraints

00:12:45.167 --> 00:12:49.833
and ensure that each plate
is treated consistently.

00:12:50.867 --> 00:12:55.233
So moving on now to flow
cytometry sample prep.

00:12:55.633 --> 00:12:58.133
There are a number of
challenges to consider

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:01.033
when preparing samples for
flow cytometry analysis.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:05.200
The first main consideration is that
you require single cell suspensions.

00:13:05.267 --> 00:13:07.600
If you're working with
an inherent cell line,

00:13:07.667 --> 00:13:10.133
this requires a trypsinization step.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:14.033
Now typically cells may often cluster
together during trypsinization,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:15.833
and this is when it's essential

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:17.800
to have something like
the multichannel head,

00:13:17.867 --> 00:13:19.700
where it can do repeated
pipetting up and down

00:13:19.767 --> 00:13:21.600
to ensure that those
clumps are broken

00:13:21.667 --> 00:13:24.633
into the single cell
suspensions that you require.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.967
Another consideration is
sample throughput,

00:13:27.033 --> 00:13:30.000
and a lot of this will be
determined by your flow cytometer.

00:13:30.067 --> 00:13:33.533
If your flow cytometer takes single
tubes or a carousel of tubes,

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:35.433
your throughput will likely be lower

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:40.200
than if your cytometer can acquire
samples using a plate-based format.

00:13:40.267 --> 00:13:43.733
However, we do have automated solutions
for both of those types of workflows.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.567
And in fact, you may be able
to process your samples in plates

00:13:46.633 --> 00:13:52.233
and then do a final transfer to tubes
if your cytometer requires it.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:54.200
Another consideration is

00:13:54.267 --> 00:13:56.767
that most of the workflows
for flow cytometry,

00:13:56.833 --> 00:13:58.967
such as antibodies sustaining workflows,

00:13:59.033 --> 00:14:01.567
require a centrifugation step
to pellet the cells

00:14:01.633 --> 00:14:04.033
in allowing you to
perform reagent exchanges

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.933
such as antibody washes.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:11.133
Another consideration is that most
of the reagents are fluorescent,

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:14.233
so this typically means
they are light sensitive,

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:15.267
so you need to consider

00:14:15.333 --> 00:14:18.500
how long you're able to leave your
reagents on the deck of your instrument.

00:14:18.567 --> 00:14:22.967
And we'll talk about each of these
requirements as we go forward.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.367
For our proof of principle experiments,

00:14:25.433 --> 00:14:28.933
we basically wanted to perform
a drug discovery screen.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:32.333
So for this, we used three
inducers of apoptosis

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:34.767
and screened two different
cancer cell lines

00:14:34.833 --> 00:14:37.567
to look at their effectiveness
both using dilution curves

00:14:37.633 --> 00:14:40.600
- so looking at different
concentrations of the drugs,

00:14:40.667 --> 00:14:44.600
and also looking at their effects
over multiple time points.

00:14:44.667 --> 00:14:47.000
We used one adherent and
one suspension cell line,

00:14:47.067 --> 00:14:50.467
the HC116 colon carcinoma cells
were adherent,

00:14:50.533 --> 00:14:54.600
and the Jurkat leukemia cell
line was our suspension example.

00:14:54.667 --> 00:14:56.400
As I mentioned before,

00:14:56.467 --> 00:14:59.033
the HCT116 cells required
that repeated pipetting

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:01.700
using the multichannel head
during the trypsinization step

00:15:01.767 --> 00:15:04.033
to ensure we had single cells.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:07.333
We then stained the cells
with markers for apoptosis

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:10.433
using a fluorescent
caspase 3/7 substrate,

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:15.333
and we also added a DRAQ7 cell
impermeable nuclear dye

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:19.833
as an indicator of cell death
looking for compromised cell membranes.

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:23.067
We then acquired our data
using the CytoFLEX flow cytometer

00:15:23.133 --> 00:15:27.767
and the system we used can acquire
15 parameters in 10 colors,

00:15:27.833 --> 00:15:30.500
although there are now even
better versions of this

00:15:30.567 --> 00:15:33.733
I think that go up to 20 or so colors.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:35.733
The nice thing about the CytoFLEX

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.767
is that it can accommodate
either tubes or plates,

00:15:37.833 --> 00:15:41.900
and obviously we went with the
plates loader for our work today.

00:15:41.967 --> 00:15:46.933
We then did the analysis using
our Kaluza G software.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.800
We'll now show a brief video

00:15:48.867 --> 00:15:51.433
that will illustrate some of the
early portion of the workflow

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:55.633
where we focus on the
treatment of the cells.

00:15:58.867 --> 00:16:03.000
This video will demonstrate
our automated flow cytometry workflow,

00:16:03.067 --> 00:16:07.000
specifically the initial compound dilution

00:16:07.067 --> 00:16:11.033
and treatment of our
cell culture plate.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:14.267
Shown here, the Span-8
system has loaded seven tips

00:16:14.333 --> 00:16:16.700
and is transferring our drug diluent

00:16:16.767 --> 00:16:18.900
into a plate that currently has

00:16:18.967 --> 00:16:22.200
the highest concentration of
our compounds in the top row.

00:16:22.267 --> 00:16:25.600
For this demonstration, we
are testing three compounds,

00:16:25.667 --> 00:16:30.033
so we will have a final dose
response curve of 24 wells each

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:34.133
with eight dilutions
of three compounds.

00:16:38.467 --> 00:16:41.467
Following this transfer, one of
the new features of the i-Series

00:16:41.533 --> 00:16:43.900
is having a universal tip type.

00:16:43.967 --> 00:16:45.800
These tips can then be shared

00:16:45.867 --> 00:16:48.667
between both the Span-8 pod
and the multichannel pod,

00:16:48.733 --> 00:16:53.000
which is very beneficial when
only using partial tip boxes.

00:16:53.067 --> 00:16:57.267
Shown next is the selective tip
feature of the multichannel head,

00:16:57.333 --> 00:16:59.967
enabling us to load just three tips,

00:17:00.033 --> 00:17:03.100
which will then be used to do
a row-wise dilution

00:17:03.167 --> 00:17:07.633
in a 1:1 fashion of our three compounds.

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:10.233
Once the serial dilution is complete,

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:13.333
as mentioned, we will
have 24 total wells,

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:17.100
eight different concentrations
of three compounds.

00:17:17.167 --> 00:17:18.933
We then load 24 tips

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:22.067
using the selective tip feature
on the multichannel head,

00:17:22.133 --> 00:17:25.933
and these compounds will then
be stamped into the cell plate

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:28.800
shown in the position behind it.

00:17:28.867 --> 00:17:30.700
By doing this repeated stamping,

00:17:30.767 --> 00:17:33.600
not only are we able to do
dose response curves,

00:17:33.667 --> 00:17:36.400
we're also able to establish
a time course of treatment

00:17:36.467 --> 00:17:39.233
by just processing partial plates.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:55.467
So now we'll look at
some of the data.

00:17:55.533 --> 00:17:58.933
Again, this was the HCT116 cells,

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:01.467
so following the
treatment of the cells,

00:18:01.533 --> 00:18:04.200
we then automated the trypsinization

00:18:04.267 --> 00:18:07.400
using the same 24 tips in
the selective tip fashion,

00:18:07.467 --> 00:18:11.967
and then added our staining
reagents and did our analysis.

00:18:12.033 --> 00:18:13.700
So looking here,

00:18:13.767 --> 00:18:17.067
we're looking at a single time
point of 48 hours after treatment,

00:18:17.133 --> 00:18:20.600
and shown on the X-axis is
our caspase 3/7 staining,

00:18:20.667 --> 00:18:23.267
and on the Y-axis is
our DRAQ7 staining.

00:18:23.333 --> 00:18:26.033
We basically picked just three
out of our eight treatments,

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:31.267
so we're representing a high, medium,
and low concentration of our compounds.

00:18:31.333 --> 00:18:34.200
In this particular case, we're
looking at 5-fluorouracil treatment.

00:18:34.267 --> 00:18:35.833
And what you can see is,

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:37.700
as you decline from
the highest concentration

00:18:37.767 --> 00:18:39.567
to the lowest concentration,

00:18:39.633 --> 00:18:44.367
the number of cells found in the
dead and apoptotic gates decease,

00:18:44.433 --> 00:18:47.567
indicating our serial dilution
was effective.

00:18:49.033 --> 00:18:52.033
In fact, we can actually plot
some nice dose response curves

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:54.233
with all of the data that we achieved.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:59.200
What you can see is that looking
at this HCT116 cell line at 48 hours,

00:18:59.267 --> 00:19:02.833
we see that staurosporine and
camptothecin were far more effective

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:05.400
at killing these cells
than 5-fluorouracil.

00:19:05.467 --> 00:19:07.600
Looking at the IC50
concentrations

00:19:07.667 --> 00:19:10.467
of 63 nanomolar, 317 nanomolars,

00:19:10.533 --> 00:19:14.167
roughly a thousand fold more
effective than the 368 micromolar

00:19:14.233 --> 00:19:20.567
required to get 50 percent cell
death in this HCT116 cell line.

00:19:20.633 --> 00:19:23.600
So again, a nice illustration
of a dose response,

00:19:23.667 --> 00:19:27.033
but we also want to look
at different time courses.

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:29.067
Shown here is our Jurkat cell line.

00:19:29.133 --> 00:19:30.600
Again, the suspension cell lines.

00:19:30.667 --> 00:19:32.200
There's no trypsinization required.

00:19:32.267 --> 00:19:35.100
We simply added our
reagents and did our analysis.

00:19:35.167 --> 00:19:38.700
This was looking at a single
concentration of camptothecin treatment

00:19:38.767 --> 00:19:40.667
at 78 nanomolar.

00:19:40.733 --> 00:19:42.500
And what you can see is, over time,

00:19:42.567 --> 00:19:46.767
the untreated cells shown in the
viable gate in the lower left quadrant,

00:19:46.833 --> 00:19:49.967
after 24 hours, move
into the apoptotic quadrant,

00:19:50.033 --> 00:19:52.700
so they're staying positive for
caspase 3/7.

00:19:52.767 --> 00:19:55.300
Still very few are seeing for DRAQ7.

00:19:55.367 --> 00:19:57.800
And then with an additional
24 and 48 hour treatments,

00:19:57.867 --> 00:20:00.500
you see more and
more percentages of the cells

00:20:00.567 --> 00:20:05.133
moving into that dead quadrant
in the upper right hand quadrant.

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:07.367
This can also be plotted out
and what you can see

00:20:07.433 --> 00:20:11.167
is a corresponding decrease
in the viable cell count at 24 hours

00:20:11.233 --> 00:20:15.133
with an increase in the
apoptotic cells,

00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:18.167
followed by a subsequent decease
in the apoptotic cells

00:20:18.233 --> 00:20:20.500
with an increase in the
number of dead cells.

00:20:20.567 --> 00:20:24.067
Again, a nice illustration
of time dependent responses

00:20:24.133 --> 00:20:27.433
in this particular cell line
as shown by flow cytometry.

00:20:29.267 --> 00:20:32.667
There's some additional consideration
when thinking about flow cytometry.

00:20:32.733 --> 00:20:34.900
We've already touched on
some of them.

00:20:34.967 --> 00:20:37.800
Some of the workflow will be
determined, again,

00:20:37.867 --> 00:20:41.067
by the flow cytometer
if you require tubes or plates.

00:20:41.133 --> 00:20:45.633
So it's nice to have the
Span-8 pod working with tubes.

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:49.567
Again, the entire workflow can be
processed in tubes using the Span-8,

00:20:49.633 --> 00:20:51.333
or like I mentioned before,

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:53.300
you could actually do the
processing on a plate

00:20:53.367 --> 00:20:55.433
and do a final transfer
with the Span-8 into the tubes

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:57.733
in a very rapid fashion.

00:20:57.800 --> 00:21:00.100
It's certainly useful to have
that multichannel head

00:21:00.167 --> 00:21:02.700
for the resuspension of adherent
cells if that's required.

00:21:02.767 --> 00:21:05.000
But again, if folks are working
on blood samples etc,

00:21:05.067 --> 00:21:08.300
that might be less of a consideration.

00:21:08.367 --> 00:21:10.900
What we showed today was
a relatively simple example

00:21:10.967 --> 00:21:14.067
of just an add and read type of assay.

00:21:14.133 --> 00:21:16.633
However, most of the more
typical workflows

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:20.800
would require antibody staining and
just more of a complex sample prep,

00:21:20.867 --> 00:21:24.067
requiring washes and reagent exchanges.

00:21:24.133 --> 00:21:28.467
These types of workflows
can definitely be aided by integrations.

00:21:28.533 --> 00:21:32.300
And shown on the lower right hand
side, we did have an i5 system

00:21:32.367 --> 00:21:35.200
that we demonstrated the integration
of the CytoFLEX itself.

00:21:35.267 --> 00:21:38.500
So if you're processing
large numbers of samples,

00:21:38.567 --> 00:21:41.133
the integration allows the analysis

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:43.567
to begin right at the completion
of sample preparing

00:21:43.633 --> 00:21:45.533
without any intervention.

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:48.100
We also, kind of on the right
hand side of that image,

00:21:48.167 --> 00:21:49.833
you can see a microplate centrifuge

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:51.967
that can be directly
integrated onto the deck.

00:21:52.033 --> 00:21:54.500
Again, this is excellent
for the spin down

00:21:54.567 --> 00:21:58.233
and wash steps for those
antibody type protocols.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:01.333
We also have the option to
integrate a plate washer,

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.367
if this would help assist things.

00:22:03.433 --> 00:22:06.133
However the multichannel can essentially
do most of this workflow by itself

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:09.400
with the addition and
removal of washes, etc.

00:22:09.467 --> 00:22:12.300
There's also the ability
to integrate incubators,

00:22:12.367 --> 00:22:15.700
either something small shown
as the single plate incubator

00:22:15.767 --> 00:22:17.700
next to the CytoFLEX in the image,

00:22:17.767 --> 00:22:24.033
or much larger, higher capacity
incubators such as a Cytomat 2 C.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:27.900
This would be good for, say,
long-term staining

00:22:27.967 --> 00:22:30.267
or just similar treatments like we had

00:22:30.333 --> 00:22:33.933
where you would treat cells
with compounds, let them incubate,

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:37.767
then bring the cells back up from
the incubator and do your staining.

00:22:37.833 --> 00:22:40.600
Once again, I've already
mentioned our SAMI EX software.

00:22:40.667 --> 00:22:44.433
This software would also be useful
for this type of application,

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:46.767
for these higher throughput methods

00:22:46.833 --> 00:22:51.100
where, again, it could process
multiple plates in staggered fashion.

00:22:51.167 --> 00:22:54.167
Once again, you can put in those
type of timing constraints

00:22:54.233 --> 00:22:56.367
for the steps that are essential.

00:22:56.433 --> 00:22:58.500
One example would be trypsinization.

00:22:58.567 --> 00:23:01.200
Obviously, you want to
consistently treat your plates

00:23:01.267 --> 00:23:03.733
because if you have longer
trypsinization times,

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:07.633
this can actually change the cell
surface expression of some proteins

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:11.700
as more of them get cleaved during
longer trypsinization treatments.

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:18.767
So I'll now move onto another example,
being transfection optimization.

00:23:18.833 --> 00:23:22.100
When I think of transfection,
it seems relatively simple.

00:23:22.167 --> 00:23:25.133
Essentially it's just combining
a lipid with a nucleic acid

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.333
and putting that
on top of cells.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:31.667
However, it's not necessary
simple for every cell type.

00:23:31.733 --> 00:23:35.133
Because of this, an optimization
approach can help.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:39.100
Unfortunately, optimizing transfection
conditions is not so simple.

00:23:39.167 --> 00:23:41.867
For this example, we chose
to use a design of experiment,

00:23:41.933 --> 00:23:43.600
or a DOE approach

00:23:43.667 --> 00:23:46.567
where we changed the different
lipids we were using,

00:23:46.633 --> 00:23:48.567
we tried different lipid concentrations,

00:23:48.633 --> 00:23:50.667
different concentrations
of nucleic acids,

00:23:50.733 --> 00:23:52.267
and different cell numbers

00:23:52.333 --> 00:23:54.267
to see if we could identify
an optimal condition

00:23:54.333 --> 00:23:57.267
for a variety of different
cell lines.

00:23:57.333 --> 00:23:59.767
The problem with this approach, or
the difficulty with this approach,

00:23:59.833 --> 00:24:02.500
is that, first off, from an
automation perspective,

00:24:02.567 --> 00:24:04.633
you need ways to
create these combinations,

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:06.700
and again, this is a nice example

00:24:06.767 --> 00:24:09.567
of how having the selective tip
feature for the multichannel head

00:24:09.633 --> 00:24:11.167
really enables some of these

00:24:11.233 --> 00:24:14.300
because you can do
row-wise or column-wise transfers

00:24:14.367 --> 00:24:17.533
as well as any
combination you might need.

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:21.633
In addition, the other problem is that
the conditions increase bacterially.

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:25.100
So very quickly, as you add
more and more conditions,

00:24:25.167 --> 00:24:27.233
the number of
samples you need to test

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:29.333
grow pretty dramatically very quickly,

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:32.667
and we'll show an
illustration of that as well.

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:34.800
So the workflow that -

00:24:34.867 --> 00:24:37.800
the approach that we took to automating
the transfection optimization is

00:24:37.867 --> 00:24:39.900
we started with an empty plate,

00:24:39.967 --> 00:24:42.233
and then tried six different
lipid conditions,

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:44.700
really five different commercial
lipids that were available,

00:24:44.767 --> 00:24:47.433
DharmaFECT one
through four and Lipofectamine,

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:50.167
and one condition that had no lipids,

00:24:50.233 --> 00:24:52.833
so essentially, our
untransfected control.

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:54.867
We used our Span-8 system

00:24:54.933 --> 00:24:59.400
to transfer these reagents from tubes
into the upper row of the 96 well plate,

00:24:59.467 --> 00:25:01.267
and then also used the Span-8

00:25:01.333 --> 00:25:06.567
to just fill in the next 18 wells
with media for serial dilutions.

00:25:06.633 --> 00:25:09.067
Shown with the dark and light
purple, we also used the Span-8

00:25:09.133 --> 00:25:12.300
to transfer two different
concentrations of our nucleic acid.

00:25:12.367 --> 00:25:15.800
We did an siRNA control.

00:25:15.867 --> 00:25:17.500
On the next step,

00:25:17.567 --> 00:25:20.367
we loaded six tips on our
multichannel selective tip,

00:25:20.433 --> 00:25:22.300
much like you saw earlier
with the three tips

00:25:22.367 --> 00:25:24.633
for the flow cytometry example,

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:27.067
and we did a serial dilution
in a row-wise fashion

00:25:27.133 --> 00:25:30.400
to get 24 different
conditions of lipids,

00:25:30.467 --> 00:25:34.667
and then stamp those into the
lower 24 wells using the same six tips.

00:25:34.733 --> 00:25:37.167
We then used our Span-8
at the same time

00:25:37.233 --> 00:25:39.500
to essentially stamp out
those nucleic acids

00:25:39.567 --> 00:25:43.167
into the remaining 48 wells
on the right hand side.

00:25:43.233 --> 00:25:46.433
We then loaded 48 tips on
the multichannel selective tip

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:50.900
and combined those 48 wells on the
left to the 48 wells on the right,

00:25:50.967 --> 00:25:55.967
resulting in 48 different conditions of
nucleic acid and lipid combinations.

00:25:56.033 --> 00:25:57.733
Following an incubation

00:25:57.800 --> 00:26:00.567
to allow for transfection
complexes to form,

00:26:00.633 --> 00:26:05.300
we then stamped those 48
conditions into a 384 well plate,

00:26:05.367 --> 00:26:07.400
and then loaded 96 tips

00:26:07.467 --> 00:26:09.900
to transfer two different
concentrations of cells,

00:26:09.967 --> 00:26:14.300
resulting in 15,000 or 7,500
cells per well.

00:26:14.367 --> 00:26:17.167
The final result was 96
different conditions -

00:26:17.233 --> 00:26:19.733
and shown in the plate map below

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:21.867
- and the four blue wells indicate

00:26:21.933 --> 00:26:26.133
the four replicate conditions
for a given condition,

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:30.300
such in this case was
.8 microliters of DharmaFECT 1

00:26:30.367 --> 00:26:36.000
with 100 nanomolars of siRNA
and 15,000 cells per well.

00:26:36.067 --> 00:26:37.700
So the nice feature here is

00:26:37.767 --> 00:26:39.600
we're able to test 96
different conditions

00:26:39.667 --> 00:26:42.767
in quadruplicate in
optimization in a single plate.

00:26:42.833 --> 00:26:44.633
If we tried to do this manually,

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:48.133
my best estimate would be that it would
take about 138 manual transfers,

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:52.000
and instead, the automation was able
to achieve this in under 20 minutes.

00:26:54.467 --> 00:26:57.133
We then tested this approach on
three different cell lines -

00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:59.767
the HCT116 cells I've already
mentioned before,

00:26:59.833 --> 00:27:04.100
and two additional cancer cell lines,
an ACHN line, which is renal carcinoma,

00:27:04.167 --> 00:27:08.633
and PANC1 pancreatic
carcinoma cell line.

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:11.933
We used siGLO green as our
transfection marker.

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.400
This is a fluorescently labeled siRNA,

00:27:14.467 --> 00:27:16.967
and 24 hours after transfection,

00:27:17.033 --> 00:27:21.733
we added our same DRAQ7 reagent
as a marker for toxicity,

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:26.300
and then we performed media exchange,
using the multichannel head again

00:27:26.367 --> 00:27:31.233
to try and reduce the amount of
background we got from the siGLO green.

00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:33.967
We then did some imaging with
the SpectraMax i3X

00:27:34.033 --> 00:27:38.033
with MiniMax 300 imaging cytometer
shown in the upper right.

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:40.967
This basically enables 4x images

00:27:41.033 --> 00:27:46.000
to be taken in bright field in
two florescent wavelengths -

00:27:46.067 --> 00:27:49.600
540 nanometers and 713 nanometers.

00:27:49.667 --> 00:27:52.600
What this enables us to do
was to count total cells

00:27:52.667 --> 00:27:55.233
and then get an idea of our
transfection efficiency

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:57.600
by counting the number of green cells,

00:27:57.667 --> 00:28:00.267
indicating our siGLO
green had been taken up.

00:28:00.333 --> 00:28:02.367
We also looked at toxicity,

00:28:02.433 --> 00:28:05.433
so the number of red nuclei divided
by the total number of cells.

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:08.733
We looked at whether we had
either inhibited cell growth

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:12.067
or whether we had lost any cells
during our media replacement

00:28:12.133 --> 00:28:16.000
by comparing our transfected
well to our untransfected wells

00:28:16.067 --> 00:28:17.767
in the bright field images.

00:28:17.833 --> 00:28:19.867
Then we also looked at
transfection consistency

00:28:19.933 --> 00:28:24.067
by looking at the CVs across
our quadruplicate wells.

00:28:25.633 --> 00:28:28.900
So no need to really look at the numbers
here but I'll basically summarize.

00:28:28.967 --> 00:28:33.200
The first set of wells
on the left, green is good.

00:28:33.267 --> 00:28:38.100
This is basically an indication of
getting over 30 percent transfection.

00:28:38.167 --> 00:28:40.433
The next set of columns, red is bad.

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:43.033
This is basically any conditions

00:28:43.100 --> 00:28:47.833
that resulted in toxicity more than
five percent above control wells.

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:50.633
The next set, brown is bad -
it's kind of difficult to see,

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:52.800
but again, this is where we had

00:28:52.867 --> 00:28:56.033
less than 80 percent of our
untransfected cell counts in the well.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:59.300
And then the final set of
columns, light green is good.

00:28:59.367 --> 00:29:03.433
This is where our transfection
CVs were below ten percent.

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:06.133
So again, the take home message here is

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:09.167
we sort of identified
the best conditions

00:29:09.233 --> 00:29:11.600
by the bold squares around those wells,

00:29:11.667 --> 00:29:14.233
and the take home here is that
you can definitely see

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:16.333
that there are differences
across the three cell lines,

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:20.267
and we'll really identify
those in the next slide.

00:29:20.333 --> 00:29:22.733
So shown here are the
resulting optimal conditions

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:24.400
for each of the cell lines,

00:29:24.467 --> 00:29:26.300
and again, what you can quickly see

00:29:26.367 --> 00:29:28.467
is that there are differences
between the cell lines.

00:29:28.533 --> 00:29:30.567
All three of them had
different ideal lipids

00:29:30.633 --> 00:29:33.267
that were used at
different concentrations,

00:29:33.333 --> 00:29:35.667
different concentrations of siRNA.

00:29:35.733 --> 00:29:37.667
But through optimization,

00:29:37.733 --> 00:29:40.000
we were able to achieve roughly
50 percent transfection efficiency

00:29:40.067 --> 00:29:44.167
with all three cell lines with
acceptable levels of toxicity,

00:29:44.233 --> 00:29:47.033
and with CVs below ten percent.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:49.667
Some of the cell lines,
like PANC1 and HCT116,

00:29:49.733 --> 00:29:53.400
were relatively transfection friendly

00:29:53.467 --> 00:29:56.300
in that there were a couple different
concentrations that might work.

00:29:56.367 --> 00:29:58.300
But the ACHN cells

00:29:58.367 --> 00:30:01.867
were significantly different and
really more troublesome to transfect,

00:30:01.933 --> 00:30:04.533
and we did have to
find that optimal condition

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:08.600
to achieve that type of
transfection efficiency.

00:30:08.667 --> 00:30:11.300
Some of the important take home
messages were that -

00:30:11.367 --> 00:30:13.600
even a simple change.

00:30:13.667 --> 00:30:16.333
Even just a twofold
change in lipid concentrations,

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:18.267
you'd see very dramatic effects.

00:30:18.333 --> 00:30:20.367
Twofold too low and you would see

00:30:20.433 --> 00:30:22.733
a very low transfection
efficiency, basically nothing.

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:25.233
Twofold too high and you would
see rampant toxicity

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:27.367
depending on your cell lines,

00:30:27.433 --> 00:30:31.500
so really driving home the need to do
this type of optimization approach.

00:30:31.567 --> 00:30:35.033
In addition, because we were
able to work in 384 well format,

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:37.700
this helped reduce our reagent usage.

00:30:37.767 --> 00:30:39.767
So again, these are not
cheap reagents,

00:30:39.833 --> 00:30:42.700
so obviously, the more you can do
in a smaller volume, the better.

00:30:42.767 --> 00:30:46.300
But to try and do this manually,
this really adds challenge to this

00:30:46.367 --> 00:30:48.333
because it's very difficult to tell

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:51.033
which wells you've added reagents to.

00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:54.000
And another nice thing is now
that we have this automated approach,

00:30:54.067 --> 00:30:57.000
it's very easily adapted
to different nucleic acids

00:30:57.067 --> 00:30:58.567
or different assays.

00:30:58.633 --> 00:31:02.467
So if you wanted to adapt this to
an overexpression type of assay,

00:31:02.533 --> 00:31:03.967
just change the reagents,

00:31:04.033 --> 00:31:06.367
possibly change your
transfection reagents

00:31:06.433 --> 00:31:10.367
and add plasmids instead of
siRNAs and it should essentially work.

00:31:10.433 --> 00:31:13.800
Alternatively, if you didn't want to
use a fluorescent siRNA as a marker,

00:31:13.867 --> 00:31:16.267
instead you had some
phenotypic readout,

00:31:16.333 --> 00:31:20.867
it might just be requiring changing
the assay approach at the end.

00:31:23.233 --> 00:31:26.733
So now we'll sort of conclude with
basic cell culture approaches.

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:30.200
Again, this is not something
everyone thinks about automating

00:31:30.267 --> 00:31:32.767
because it's something that was
typically done at the bench,

00:31:32.833 --> 00:31:36.400
but this really is something that
can benefit from automation.

00:31:36.467 --> 00:31:38.233
There's a number of reasons for that

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:41.100
because there are a number of challenges
associated with cell culture.

00:31:41.167 --> 00:31:43.767
The first is that it's
basically very time-consuming.

00:31:43.833 --> 00:31:46.067
And this can be because
it recurs frequently;

00:31:46.133 --> 00:31:48.967
you're probably passaging cells
two to three times a week.

00:31:49.033 --> 00:31:51.333
If you have multiple cell lines
going at the same time,

00:31:51.400 --> 00:31:53.500
the throughput can become
really burdensome.

00:31:53.567 --> 00:31:55.767
And it's relatively inflexible.

00:31:55.833 --> 00:31:58.400
Cells don't really care if you
have an all-day meeting schedule.

00:31:58.467 --> 00:32:01.733
If they're confluent,
they need to be passed that day.

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:05.300
In addition, there's high
variability in this type of work.

00:32:05.367 --> 00:32:07.600
For example, sometimes
you just have cells

00:32:07.667 --> 00:32:09.600
that you're waiting to plate
later in the week

00:32:09.667 --> 00:32:11.767
and so maybe you don't have
that much work.

00:32:11.833 --> 00:32:13.967
But Monday may look very
different than Thursday,

00:32:14.200 --> 00:32:17.667
or this week may look
very different than next week.

00:32:17.733 --> 00:32:19.933
In addition, we know
there are pepe in the lab

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.300
who passage cells differently than I do.

00:32:22.367 --> 00:32:24.500
So for example,

00:32:24.567 --> 00:32:28.133
we all sort of come up with our
own approach for passaging cells,

00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:30.167
and it just depends
on how we were taught

00:32:30.233 --> 00:32:32.367
or maybe what the written protocol says,

00:32:32.500 --> 00:32:35.667
but we know there's inherent variability
in the way people do things.

00:32:35.767 --> 00:32:37.567
By turning to automation,

00:32:37.633 --> 00:32:39.833
this variability can go away
because a lot for the times,

00:32:39.900 --> 00:32:43.700
this variability will show up
in your future assays.

00:32:44.367 --> 00:32:46.633
And finally, it comes down
to scheduling as well -

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:47.533
when do I need cells?

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:49.400
When am I going to
plate my experiments?

00:32:49.467 --> 00:32:51.467
When I'm doing multi-day workflows,

00:32:51.533 --> 00:32:55.900
frequently some of these cell culture
assays may take days to weeks.

00:32:55.967 --> 00:32:58.167
What planning ahead do I need to do

00:32:58.233 --> 00:33:00.900
to ensure that I'm not here
all day on a weekend?

00:33:00.967 --> 00:33:03.033
So these are just some
of the considerations

00:33:03.100 --> 00:33:06.067
that can hopefully be
addressed through automation.

00:33:06.133 --> 00:33:09.367
For the work we've done,
this will be done on an i7,

00:33:09.433 --> 00:33:12.367
same as most of the work
has been done previously,

00:33:12.433 --> 00:33:15.933
and it features a number of
integrations that we will show shortly.

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:19.000
The work we did involved just
culturally two adherent cell lines,

00:33:19.067 --> 00:33:21.433
CHO cells, which we'll show
most of the data for,

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:23.767
as well as HCT116,

00:33:23.833 --> 00:33:27.133
and a suspension cell
line, the Jurkat cells.

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:29.833
Basically, we're passaging
the cells in six well plates,

00:33:29.900 --> 00:33:33.733
so large well plates to have enough
cells to plate for experiments.

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:37.067
We would pass a known cell
number every two to three days,

00:33:37.133 --> 00:33:39.067
and then we would plate these cells

00:33:39.233 --> 00:33:42.267
in either 96 or 384 well plates
for future assays

00:33:42.367 --> 00:33:44.500
as a proof of principle.

00:33:44.567 --> 00:33:47.867
The system itself utilized and
integrated Cytomat 2 C incubator

00:33:47.933 --> 00:33:50.333
to allow us to culture
our cells directly

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:53.267
and bring them onto the system
in an automated fashion.

00:33:53.333 --> 00:33:56.600
It also had an integrated bi-cell
XR cell viability analyzer,

00:33:56.667 --> 00:33:58.500
shown on the right,

00:33:58.567 --> 00:34:01.933
and this would enable us to
count our cells at time of passaging.

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:04.633
It also had a tilting
ALP for larger well plates

00:34:04.700 --> 00:34:07.433
to basically allow the media
to slide all to one side

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:10.433
and enable us to remove that media
without disturbing the cells.

00:34:10.500 --> 00:34:13.200
And we also had the
integrated SpectraMax i3X

00:34:13.267 --> 00:34:15.300
with MiniMax that I mentioned earlier

00:34:15.367 --> 00:34:17.833
and we used this for some imaging
and cell counting assays,

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:19.900
which we'll describe later.

00:34:20.700 --> 00:34:22.500
Now we'll show a video,

00:34:22.567 --> 00:34:25.333
again, that will really demonstrate
how this workflow proceeds

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:29.100
and how we utilize the different
integrated instruments.

00:34:37.467 --> 00:34:42.233
So shown here is the automated approach
we took to automating cell culture,

00:34:42.300 --> 00:34:44.633
starting from the larger six well plates

00:34:44.700 --> 00:34:48.033
and moving assays into 96 well formats.

00:34:49.400 --> 00:34:50.667
Shown here is the tilting ALP,

00:34:50.733 --> 00:34:52.533
and the advantage of this is

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:55.000
that the media can be removed
from the edge of the well

00:34:55.067 --> 00:34:58.100
without disturbing the
adherent cells on the well.

00:34:58.167 --> 00:35:00.667
We used the Span-8 system
with a P1000

00:35:00.733 --> 00:35:03.367
to reach into the corner
well and remove the media,

00:35:03.433 --> 00:35:07.567
and then another P1000 tip to
dispense 800 microliters of trypsin

00:35:07.633 --> 00:35:11.433
to remove the cells from
the surface of the plate.

00:35:11.500 --> 00:35:15.167
This plate was then incubated at
37 degrees for five minutes,

00:35:15.233 --> 00:35:17.467
and this was done on aesthetic Peltier,

00:35:17.533 --> 00:35:21.500
but could've easily been done on
the integrated incubator as well.

00:35:21.567 --> 00:35:23.300
We then used our optimized pipetting

00:35:23.367 --> 00:35:25.967
to pipette around the
circumference of the well

00:35:26.033 --> 00:35:29.833
to make sure the cells were removed
from the surface of the plate.

00:35:29.967 --> 00:35:32.167
And then also did a mixing step

00:35:32.233 --> 00:35:34.300
to ensure that the cells
were distributed

00:35:34.367 --> 00:35:36.467
into a single cell suspension.

00:35:37.067 --> 00:35:40.933
A portion of these re-suspended
cells were then added to the sample cup

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:44.900
for the integrated bi-cells
cell viability analyzer,

00:35:44.967 --> 00:35:48.600
and the bi-cell returned a
viable cells per mil number,

00:35:48.667 --> 00:35:51.567
which the Biomek was
automatically able to use

00:35:51.633 --> 00:35:54.733
to calculate what volume of
cells needed to be transferred

00:35:54.800 --> 00:35:57.733
to reach a certain number of cells.

00:35:57.800 --> 00:36:00.967
If the volume was over one mil,
the capacity of the tip,

00:36:01.033 --> 00:36:03.200
the split volume step
could be used

00:36:03.267 --> 00:36:06.567
to automatically transfer
the larger volume.

00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:08.867
In this case, we dispensed
one million cells

00:36:08.933 --> 00:36:12.267
into the subsequent well
for continuing passages.

00:36:12.333 --> 00:36:14.767
And a second subset of cells
was then added

00:36:14.833 --> 00:36:19.300
to a large multichannel reservoir
containing 100 mils of media.

00:36:19.367 --> 00:36:22.700
One of the advantages of
the large capacity multichannel head

00:36:22.767 --> 00:36:26.933
is that we're now able to completely
mix these large volume reservoirs

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:31.767
and ensure the cells were evenly
distributed throughout the reservoir.

00:36:32.033 --> 00:36:34.600
In addition, we were able
to increase the throughput

00:36:34.667 --> 00:36:36.933
by using a multi-dispense feature,

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:41.900
allowing us to plate 496 well
plates with 200 microliters per well

00:36:41.967 --> 00:36:44.000
in a single transfer step.

00:36:48.533 --> 00:36:51.267
Again, this can be used to
increase the throughout

00:36:51.333 --> 00:36:53.800
and process numerous
plates simultaneously

00:36:53.867 --> 00:36:57.733
or process as many plates in a
subsequent fashion as needed.

00:37:09.800 --> 00:37:12.300
So hopefully that gives you
a pretty good idea

00:37:12.367 --> 00:37:14.867
of what the automation looks like
and what the workflow looks like.

00:37:14.933 --> 00:37:18.567
We'll now look at some of the
data that came off this system.

00:37:18.633 --> 00:37:20.333
So the first question is

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:23.367
just regarding the passaging
from one six well to another.

00:37:23.433 --> 00:37:25.400
So all the work we'll
show here was done

00:37:25.533 --> 00:37:29.167
using the Chinese hamster ovary
cells grown in adherent conditions.

00:37:29.233 --> 00:37:35.100
Demonstrated here is basically the
results of one of our six well passages.

00:37:35.167 --> 00:37:39.467
Shown below is basically taking
four regions of interest

00:37:39.533 --> 00:37:42.233
and taking 12 images for each region,

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:47.867
using the SpectraMax MinMax imaging
cytometer 24 hours after plating.

00:37:47.933 --> 00:37:51.833
And what you can see is that there's
no clumps of cells or no clusters,

00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:56.000
so it looks like we had really
effective trypsinization in mixing,

00:37:56.067 --> 00:37:59.500
and also, it looks very evenly
distributed across the four regions.

00:37:59.567 --> 00:38:02.200
So what you can see on the left
is what I could achieve manually,

00:38:02.267 --> 00:38:05.900
and on the right is what the system was
able to do in an automated fashion.

00:38:05.967 --> 00:38:08.667
We got very comparable
percentages of confluence,

00:38:08.733 --> 00:38:10.633
right around 60 percent,

00:38:10.700 --> 00:38:12.933
and very low CVs in both cases,

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:16.100
indicating good dispersion
across the wells.

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:20.300
Looking across multiple passages now,

00:38:20.367 --> 00:38:24.767
so shown here are basically
the automated comparison and manual

00:38:24.833 --> 00:38:26.733
of what I was able to achieve.

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:28.833
And we look on the left
at cell counts,

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:32.600
and looking at how consistent
those were across passages

00:38:32.667 --> 00:38:35.400
and also across
automation versus manual.

00:38:35.467 --> 00:38:38.133
And on the right hand side
is plotting viability.

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:40.167
So we were able to achieve

00:38:40.233 --> 00:38:45.400
consistently above 90 percent viability
in all six of our automated passages.

00:38:45.467 --> 00:38:47.867
More interesting is that
actually if you look,

00:38:47.933 --> 00:38:51.367
we were able to achieve results
that are more consistent

00:38:51.433 --> 00:38:53.633
than I was able to achieve manually.

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:57.100
So again, looking at CVs of 7.9
percent in an automated fashion

00:38:57.167 --> 00:39:00.267
when comparing cell counts
across six passages

00:39:00.333 --> 00:39:04.567
was better than the 13.8 percent CVs
I was able to achieve manually.

00:39:04.633 --> 00:39:08.233
We also noticed that there was
slightly less cells on average

00:39:08.300 --> 00:39:10.167
in the automated well.

00:39:10.233 --> 00:39:13.033
And that's likely because when we
have the plate in a titled fashion,

00:39:13.100 --> 00:39:15.100
I can't actually angle the pipette

00:39:15.167 --> 00:39:17.400
the way I can when I'm
performing it manually.

00:39:17.467 --> 00:39:19.267
So there's that small five to ten -

00:39:19.333 --> 00:39:21.467
probably five percent of the well

00:39:21.533 --> 00:39:24.233
that may not necessarily get rinsed
down during the automated passage.

00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:26.233
So we're likely plating
the same number of cells

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:28.100
and culturing the same number of cells,

00:39:28.167 --> 00:39:30.167
but can't quite harvest
the same number of cells.

00:39:30.233 --> 00:39:32.300
But again, we can do
this more consistently

00:39:32.367 --> 00:39:34.367
and this consistency
can hopefully lead

00:39:34.433 --> 00:39:37.700
to more consistent results
with our assays.

00:39:37.767 --> 00:39:39.933
When it comes to actually plating,

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:43.100
the video demonstrated plating
into 496 well plates,

00:39:43.167 --> 00:39:45.567
but we actually decided to
take it one step further

00:39:45.633 --> 00:39:48.633
and try and plate into
two 384 well plates,

00:39:48.700 --> 00:39:51.200
doing 100 microliters multi-dispenses,

00:39:51.267 --> 00:39:53.700
much like we demonstrated in the video.

00:39:53.767 --> 00:39:59.200
So again, 100 microliters
per well times 768 wells.

00:39:59.267 --> 00:40:03.567
What we were able to see is that we
did, again, imaging after 24 hours.

00:40:03.633 --> 00:40:05.567
We then looked to see,

00:40:05.633 --> 00:40:08.433
did we get consistent distribution
of the cells across the plates?

00:40:08.500 --> 00:40:11.267
So essentially, did our
mixing in the reservoir work?

00:40:11.333 --> 00:40:16.933
Did we get roughly equivalent cells
in all regions of the plate?

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:20.667
[AUDIO CUTS]

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:37.367
Finally, we looked at how consistent
was our multi-dispense?

00:40:37.433 --> 00:40:40.300
Basically, were we getting the same
number of cells for each dispense

00:40:40.367 --> 00:40:42.767
in all of the eight quadrants?

00:40:42.833 --> 00:40:45.233
And when we compared
the average counts per quadrant,

00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:47.567
we saw CVs less than seven percent.

00:40:47.633 --> 00:40:51.000
So again, a very consistent
passaging and plating,

00:40:51.067 --> 00:40:55.967
hopefully resulting in
consistent assay results.

00:40:58.267 --> 00:41:02.067
Another consideration that we touched
on earlier is multi-day workflows.

00:41:02.133 --> 00:41:05.767
For example, we've just plated
our 96 or 384 well plates.

00:41:05.833 --> 00:41:08.000
How long do these plates
need to incubate?

00:41:08.067 --> 00:41:09.733
Is there an additional treatment?

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:11.633
How long until we assay them?

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:14.200
These things all have to be
taken into consideration

00:41:14.267 --> 00:41:17.167
because very rarely is this
a single day approach.

00:41:17.233 --> 00:41:19.867
So when automating these
types of approaches,

00:41:19.933 --> 00:41:21.933
there's a number of requirements.

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:23.500
One main one is that
you need to have

00:41:23.567 --> 00:41:25.800
good resource management
and scheduling,

00:41:25.933 --> 00:41:27.600
and this comes back to
our SAMI EX software

00:41:27.667 --> 00:41:29.700
where, again, when you're
processing multiple plates,

00:41:29.767 --> 00:41:31.500
it will find the most efficient way

00:41:31.667 --> 00:41:34.467
to process those plates
in badge-wise fashion.

00:41:34.533 --> 00:41:36.500
It also requires sample tracking.

00:41:36.567 --> 00:41:39.633
You need to know where your samples are
at any given time in the workflow.

00:41:39.700 --> 00:41:41.467
And you also need to know

00:41:41.533 --> 00:41:43.867
if your Biomek will be
available on a given day.

00:41:43.933 --> 00:41:46.167
So if you have interleaved
multi-day workflows,

00:41:46.233 --> 00:41:50.633
our SAMI process management software can
help fill these things on a calendar

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:54.300
so you can ensure that, again,
will you need to come in on a weekend?

00:41:54.367 --> 00:41:58.300
Is the instrument already being
taken up for other assays on a given day?

00:41:58.367 --> 00:42:00.367
And finally, you need
good data tracking,

00:42:00.433 --> 00:42:03.200
and this is where our data acquisition
and reporting tool, DART,

00:42:03.267 --> 00:42:05.467
comes into account.

00:42:05.533 --> 00:42:08.333
Basically these types
of multi-day assays

00:42:08.400 --> 00:42:10.767
may be made up
of little islands of automation

00:42:10.833 --> 00:42:13.633
where you'll bring it in on day
zero, seed the cells,

00:42:13.700 --> 00:42:16.000
send them to an incubator,
bring them back on day three,

00:42:16.067 --> 00:42:17.733
treat them.

00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:20.467
DART is basically our tool that
every time that play comes in,

00:42:20.533 --> 00:42:22.267
it can read the barcode

00:42:22.333 --> 00:42:24.567
and know everything that's been
done to that plate to date,

00:42:24.633 --> 00:42:26.633
and all of that information is
carried forward,

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:29.833
so that eventually your treatment
and your eventual assay result

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:33.433
will all be located on the same
database on the same well.

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:39.600
So to wrap up the automated
cell culture portion of the talk today,

00:42:39.667 --> 00:42:41.467
hopefully I've shown you

00:42:41.533 --> 00:42:44.333
that we were able to handle
larger well plates and flasks

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:46.300
really using the span capabilities,

00:42:46.367 --> 00:42:48.667
but having that
combination of pipetters.

00:42:48.733 --> 00:42:51.333
The span for those six well
plates and the multichannel

00:42:51.400 --> 00:42:52.600
for the micrometer plates

00:42:52.667 --> 00:42:54.767
really is valuable for
this type of workflow.

00:42:54.833 --> 00:42:56.433
There are some caveats.

00:42:56.500 --> 00:42:58.633
One is that you may need
to do some optimization

00:42:58.700 --> 00:43:00.600
for any given new cell line.

00:43:00.667 --> 00:43:03.067
One example is the
frequency of passaging.

00:43:03.133 --> 00:43:05.500
For example, when we were
passing the Jurkat cells,

00:43:05.567 --> 00:43:08.433
we would pass them every two days
and they were completely happy.

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:10.933
The CHO cells, when we tried to
passage them every two days,

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:12.867
seemed to actually die off a little bit

00:43:12.933 --> 00:43:15.300
and that's why we
batched it off to three days.

00:43:15.367 --> 00:43:17.167
Another consideration is

00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:20.767
the trypsinization workflows
for adherent cells.

00:43:20.867 --> 00:43:23.167
One is just the trypsinization time.

00:43:23.233 --> 00:43:26.100
Five minutes of incubation worked
great for the CHO cell lines,

00:43:26.167 --> 00:43:29.900
but the HCT116 cells were
a little more aggregated,

00:43:29.967 --> 00:43:31.800
so you might want to consider

00:43:31.867 --> 00:43:34.367
extending that time closer
to seven to eight minutes.

00:43:34.433 --> 00:43:37.567
In addition, the volume that you
transfer into the next well

00:43:37.633 --> 00:43:40.200
is somewhat dependent
on just your cell size.

00:43:40.267 --> 00:43:42.833
If you have a confluent
HCT116 well,

00:43:42.900 --> 00:43:46.933
you can fit about five million cells
per well as opposed to the CHO cells,

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:50.000
where a confluent well
only had 2.5 times 10:6.

00:43:50.067 --> 00:43:53.033
So if you were transferring a
million cells into the next well,

00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:55.000
and you were concerned
about residual trypsin,

00:43:55.067 --> 00:43:58.300
you're actually transferring twice the
amount of volume in the CHO cells

00:43:58.367 --> 00:44:00.700
as you are on the HCT116.

00:44:00.767 --> 00:44:04.633
So if you do find a need to either
spin out any of those reagents,

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:07.300
obviously there are some automated
solutions for that as well

00:44:07.367 --> 00:44:09.067
and you can do media exchanges

00:44:09.133 --> 00:44:11.433
and not have to worry
about residual trypsin.

00:44:11.500 --> 00:44:13.800
And finally it comes down
to mixed steps as well.

00:44:13.867 --> 00:44:15.067
As I mentioned, the HC116 cells

00:44:15.133 --> 00:44:18.833
were a tale more clumping
than the CHO cell were

00:44:18.900 --> 00:44:23.200
and so we basically increased the
number of mixes that we performed.

00:44:23.267 --> 00:44:25.567
Another consideration is
the plating needs.

00:44:25.633 --> 00:44:27.633
One of the nice features
of the Biomek is

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:29.767
that you're able to do
data-driven decisions.

00:44:29.833 --> 00:44:32.700
For example, if your cells haven't grown
as much as you thought they might

00:44:32.867 --> 00:44:35.467
and you're already in
the middle of passaging them,

00:44:35.533 --> 00:44:37.767
you can actually just have
some if/then statements

00:44:37.833 --> 00:44:39.567
indicating that if you don't have

00:44:39.633 --> 00:44:42.233
a high enough number of cells
to do your cell plating,

00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:44.200
you can just go ahead
and passage them

00:44:44.267 --> 00:44:47.733
and push it off for another couple
days without wasting all the plastic.

00:44:49.633 --> 00:44:51.867
So that basically kind of
wraps up the talk.

00:44:51.933 --> 00:44:54.233
We'll bring it back
to the simplicity, efficiency,

00:44:54.300 --> 00:44:56.267
adaptability, and reliability,

00:44:56.333 --> 00:44:59.000
and see if we've hopefully
shown some examples

00:44:59.067 --> 00:45:01.700
of how these apply
to cellular applications.

00:45:01.767 --> 00:45:03.467
When it comes to simplicity,

00:45:03.533 --> 00:45:05.667
having that volume splitting feature

00:45:05.733 --> 00:45:08.367
where, again, it really doesn't
necessarily care

00:45:08.433 --> 00:45:10.633
what the volume is
you need to transfer,

00:45:10.700 --> 00:45:12.033
you don't necessarily need to put in

00:45:12.100 --> 00:45:14.700
any sort of conditional
statements or scripting.

00:45:14.767 --> 00:45:16.800
Whatever the results from
the bi-cell come back,

00:45:16.867 --> 00:45:18.867
we can always just basically
have a transfer -

00:45:19.000 --> 00:45:23.200
split the number of transfers it
needs to get the correct volume.

00:45:23.267 --> 00:45:26.933
Also, having the integration,
we can be more direct.

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:29.867
Obviously, you can fit a lot of
instruments in a smaller footprint,

00:45:29.933 --> 00:45:31.933
and not necessarily requiring

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:34.900
any additional robotic
transports to access these devices,

00:45:34.967 --> 00:45:39.533
really increases the simplicity of
those types of integrated systems.

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:42.267
When it comes to efficiency,
we have the high capacity deck.

00:45:42.333 --> 00:45:44.300
There's just more room,
more real estate to work with,

00:45:44.367 --> 00:45:48.100
so you can really have these more
highly complex workflows as you need.

00:45:48.167 --> 00:45:51.333
It can all fit potentially
on to the same deck.

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:54.033
And there's also the ability to
do the large volume transfer

00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:57.967
so the multichannel head as we
demonstrated for the plating video.

00:45:58.033 --> 00:46:00.833
And obviously, this can really just
make it a more efficient workflow;

00:46:00.900 --> 00:46:04.267
you can process more plates
in a more rapid fashion.

00:46:04.333 --> 00:46:06.033
When it comes to adaptability,

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:08.367
hopefully I've shown that
we were able to automate

00:46:08.433 --> 00:46:11.833
a pretty diverse number of
workflows on the same system.

00:46:11.900 --> 00:46:13.933
Really kind of indicating that,

00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:17.300
yes, there really can be adapted
to pretty much any need you have,

00:46:17.467 --> 00:46:20.067
and also the ability to use the
multichannel selective tip

00:46:20.133 --> 00:46:24.533
really does increase the flexibility
of that multichannel head.

00:46:24.600 --> 00:46:28.267
So again, you're able to do more
with that head alone.

00:46:28.333 --> 00:46:31.400
And when it comes to reliability,
obviously having the remote monitoring.

00:46:31.467 --> 00:46:34.300
Some of these cellular
applications, the ELISA in particular,

00:46:34.367 --> 00:46:36.300
can take multiple hours.

00:46:36.367 --> 00:46:38.633
So if you want to just be able to
check on your system,

00:46:38.700 --> 00:46:40.133
it shouldn't still be running,

00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:42.267
but again, being able to monitor
what's happening remotely

00:46:42.333 --> 00:46:44.633
does provide you that
extra sense of security.

00:46:44.700 --> 00:46:46.700
And having our PROService,

00:46:46.767 --> 00:46:50.567
being able to access any sort of
error logs, or those video files,

00:46:50.633 --> 00:46:53.467
really should be able to help
decrease any downtime

00:46:53.533 --> 00:46:55.433
that the system might have.

00:46:56.333 --> 00:46:58.033
So with that, I'll wrap up.

00:46:58.100 --> 00:47:00.100
Obviously we have more
information available.

00:47:00.167 --> 00:47:02.300
I've just kind of touched on
some of the applications,

00:47:02.367 --> 00:47:04.233
but if you want more detail,

00:47:04.300 --> 00:47:06.967
such as the volumes we were
using or the specific reagents,

00:47:07.033 --> 00:47:10.333
that type of information can be
found in our application notes,

00:47:10.400 --> 00:47:12.967
as well as a variety of other
resources that can be found

00:47:13.033 --> 00:47:15.433
at biomek.beckman.com.

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:18.933
And with that, I'm happy to
take any questions.

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:21.967
Thank you, Dr. Kowalski, for
that informative presentation.

00:47:22.033 --> 00:47:23.600
It is time for Q &amp; A.

00:47:23.667 --> 00:47:26.867
If you have a question you'd like
to ask Dr. Kowalski, please do so now.

00:47:26.933 --> 00:47:28.833
Just click on the green Q &amp; A button

00:47:28.900 --> 00:47:30.800
at the lower left of the
Presentation window,

00:47:30.867 --> 00:47:33.167
type your question into the
box that appears your screen,

00:47:33.233 --> 00:47:35.067
and click on the Send button.

00:47:35.133 --> 00:47:37.567
We'll answer as many questions
as we have time for.

00:47:37.633 --> 00:47:40.700
The first question is,
"Have you successfully automated

00:47:40.767 --> 00:47:45.667
cell culture without the titling
ALP by optimizing pipetting?"

00:47:45.833 --> 00:47:47.233
That's a great question

00:47:47.300 --> 00:47:51.367
and again, it might necessarily
depend on a given workflow.

00:47:51.433 --> 00:47:55.000
For example, the large one well
plates, as they're called,

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:59.233
that would potentially be a little trickier
to kind of work our way around.

00:47:59.300 --> 00:48:00.867
But the six well plate,

00:48:00.933 --> 00:48:02.633
we are able to aspirate around
the circumference,

00:48:02.700 --> 00:48:05.800
and that would be just as effective
way of removing the media

00:48:05.867 --> 00:48:07.467
without disturbing the cells.

00:48:07.533 --> 00:48:10.367
It might just take a little longer
to kind of execute that process.

00:48:10.433 --> 00:48:13.133
So there's definitely a time and
a place for the tilting ALP

00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:14.933
where it provides value,

00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:19.000
but certainly not required for most
of the plates up to the very large,

00:48:19.167 --> 00:48:22.033
maybe one well plates or,
for example, the auto-flasks,

00:48:22.100 --> 00:48:24.833
which really only have a
single point of entry,

00:48:24.900 --> 00:48:27.567
that's where, again, the
tilting ALP might be required.

00:48:27.633 --> 00:48:29.933
Otherwise, it may just have
benefits for some of the plates,

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:32.600
but probably isn't necessary.

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:37.600
"How hard is it to adapt the
automation to different workflows?"

00:48:37.667 --> 00:48:39.700
That's also a good question.

00:48:39.767 --> 00:48:44.167
So the workflows I've shown today
were all done using the same deck,

00:48:44.233 --> 00:48:47.267
but obviously it depends what
system you'd be working on.

00:48:47.333 --> 00:48:51.633
Having the i7 hybrid sort of provides
you with the most flexibility,

00:48:51.733 --> 00:48:54.967
but there are certain occasions where,
again, depending on your workflow,

00:48:55.033 --> 00:48:57.300
if things are changing dramatically,

00:48:57.367 --> 00:48:59.633
you can imagine possibly needed

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:02.633
to change what devices are
integrated to the system.

00:49:02.700 --> 00:49:04.800
One of the nice things
on the i-Series is, again,

00:49:04.867 --> 00:49:07.433
there's more universality
of the integration,

00:49:07.500 --> 00:49:10.100
so those do become more easily
swapped in and out.

00:49:10.167 --> 00:49:13.767
But when it comes to just changing the
positions on the deck, for example,

00:49:13.833 --> 00:49:17.400
if you needed more heating and cooling
positions or more shaking positions,

00:49:17.467 --> 00:49:21.167
or have some direct integrations
that change to your deck configuration,

00:49:21.233 --> 00:49:23.067
that's very easily done.

00:49:23.133 --> 00:49:26.167
That's basically just popping
out the current ALP positions,

00:49:26.233 --> 00:49:28.733
putting on the next positions,

00:49:28.800 --> 00:49:34.233
and then doing a quick auto frame
using our tools that are available.

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:36.533
When it comes to possibly
swapping out heads, for example,

00:49:36.600 --> 00:49:40.367
you can change between the 96
and the 384 multichannel head.

00:49:40.433 --> 00:49:43.133
That process is basically just
unscrewing four screws

00:49:43.200 --> 00:49:44.933
and screwing them back in.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:48.500
So even changing for that type
of workflow is very rapid.

00:49:48.567 --> 00:49:53.533
It's basically built and designed to
have high levels of flexibility,

00:49:53.600 --> 00:49:57.567
so again, barring some massive
reconstruction of your needs,

00:49:57.767 --> 00:50:02.433
it should be very quickly able to
switch between workflows,

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:05.767
or otherwise you may be able
to just automate a number of workflows

00:50:05.833 --> 00:50:07.367
on a single configuration.

00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:11.100
"How do you handle contamination?"

00:50:11.167 --> 00:50:13.933
So this comes up a lot, actually.

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:17.767
In seven years of doing this,
I've only run into it once,

00:50:17.833 --> 00:50:22.500
and my way of dealing with it was to
throw out the contaminated plate.

00:50:22.567 --> 00:50:24.833
I really didn't have any other problems.

00:50:24.900 --> 00:50:26.900
That's kind one of the benefits

00:50:26.967 --> 00:50:29.000
that, in theory, if things are
working right,

00:50:29.067 --> 00:50:30.767
you shouldn't really be
spreading contamination,

00:50:30.833 --> 00:50:32.567
so if you want to treat it

00:50:32.633 --> 00:50:35.033
the way I would treat a
contaminated incubator,

00:50:35.100 --> 00:50:37.133
you can spray things down with ethanol.

00:50:37.200 --> 00:50:39.600
But just throwing away the
plate was sufficient for me.

00:50:39.667 --> 00:50:41.933
I didn't really have any
continuing problems after that.

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:45.067
There was not really any
need to do anything extreme.

00:50:45.133 --> 00:50:46.967
But there are certainly -

00:50:47.033 --> 00:50:49.633
you know, we do have information
on chemical compatibility,

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:52.700
if people really need more
information about sterilization.

00:50:52.767 --> 00:50:56.900
But honestly, for me, everything stays
pretty well-contained within the plate,

00:50:56.967 --> 00:51:00.333
so throwing it out took care
of the problem.

00:51:00.400 --> 00:51:02.500
As we're waiting for additional
questions to come in,

00:51:02.567 --> 00:51:06.033
I would like to once again thank
Dr. Kowalski for his presentation.

00:51:06.100 --> 00:51:08.700
Do you have any final comments?

